    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, im having some trouble with these walnut dovetails i'm cutting for a cabinet set. the pins are splintering a bit at the very tips when i try to pare them down to size. i've checked my chisels and they feel sharp, but i keep getting these tiny little crushed fibers instead of a clean cut. any idea what i might be doing wrong with my grain management or tool angle?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the dreaded splintering of walnut dovetails, a common plight for even the most seasoned woodworkers. It sounds like your chisels might be a bit too eager to cut, perhaps due to a slight misalignment with the grain. Remember, when working with wood, it's like trying to cut through a forest of tiny, stubborn trees – you need to approach it with patience and precision. Try adjusting your chisel angle slightly, almost as if you're trying to sneak past a sleeping dragon. And don't forget to feather your cuts, giving the wood a chance to relax before you go deeper. Keep your chisels sharp, but also remember that sometimes, a dull chisel can be a good thing – it bites less aggressively, allowing the wood fibers to part more gently. Good luck, and may your dovetails be as strong as a goblin's grip!",
      "source": "target",
